# DOS FILES ARE CRLF, BYTE FOR BYTE. Anything a 1990s DOS tool reads or IS.
#
# `-text` tells git to store exactly what is on disk and never normalise it. A
# reconstruction measured by byte comparison against a 1993 executable cannot
# let a checkout rewrite a byte of the files TPC, TASM or DOSBox's shell read.
#
# This is not enforcement on its own -- eolcheck.py exists because that same
# rule was documented, the extension was listed, and a bare-LF .BAT still
# shipped and made DOSBox report a program as missing while it sat in the same
# directory. The attribute stops git from changing a file; the check finds a
# file that was written wrong in the first place.
*.PAS  -text
*.ASM  -text
*.INC  -text
*.BAT  -text
*.CFG  -text
*.MAP  -text
*.TPU  -text
*.OBJ  -text
*.OVL  -text
*.EXE  -text
*.DAT  -text
*.MOD  -text
*.bin  -text

# Documents are UTF-8 and LF, which is the other half of encaudit.py's
# convention.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
